---
// Privacy Policy. Every statement here mirrors the network audit of the app
// (issue #58). When the app starts sending, storing or receiving anything
// else, re-run the audit first, then change this page.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Marker from '../components/Marker.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="What data Pocket keeps on your Mac, what it sends, and who receives it."
>
  <section class="container page">
    <Marker letter="P" play />
    <h1 class="h indent">Privacy</h1>
    <div class="indent prose">
    <p class="label muted">Last updated: November 13, 2026</p>

    <p>
      This page explains what Pocket, a movable, resizable widget for the Mac, does with your
      data: what stays on your Mac, what is sent, and who receives it.
    </p>

    <h2>In short</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Pocket has no account, no analytics, no ads and no crash reporting.</li>
      <li>
        Our own server receives one thing: a hashed ID of your Mac. It stores that hash and the
        date your Trial started.
      </li>
      <li>
        Lemon Squeezy, which sells Pocket for us, receives your License key and the same hashed
        Mac ID when you activate a License.
      </li>
      <li>
        Mail goes between your Mac and your own mail provider. The Assistant sends what you ask
        to the AI provider you connect. Neither passes through our servers.
      </li>
      <li>
        A few features contact other companies: Gravatar for sender pictures, Spotify for album
        covers, and Apple for dictation in some languages. The details are below.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      Pocket is made and sold by dk2la, an individual based in France, who is the data
      controller for the data described here. For any privacy question, write to
      <a href="mailto:dk2la@icloud.com">dk2la@icloud.com</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What stays on your Mac</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Clipboard history</strong> is kept in memory only. It is never written to disk
        and never sent anywhere.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Calendar.</strong> Pocket reads and edits your calendar through macOS. Pocket
        does not upload it. An event you create or change is synced by macOS to the calendar
        accounts you have set up, the same as in the Calendar app.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Notes</strong> are plain files in a folder you choose. Pocket does not upload
        them. If that folder is inside iCloud Drive or another synced location, that service
        syncs the files.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Files, timers, system stats, keyboard shortcuts, window layouts and
        settings</strong> are handled on your Mac and make no network requests.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Music controls</strong> talk to the Spotify and Music apps on your Mac. The one
        exception is album covers for Spotify, described below.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The Assistant is the exception to this list: when you use it, some of this information
      can be sent to your AI provider. See <a href="#assistant">Assistant</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Our Trial server</h2>
    <p>
      To run the 3-day Trial, Pocket sends one value to our Trial server: a hashed ID of your
      Mac. It is a one-way hash (SHA-256) of your Mac's hardware ID, so the hardware ID cannot
      be read back from it. It is still a stable identifier for your Mac.
    </p>
    <p>
      The server stores that hash and the date and time your Trial started. Nothing else: no
      name, no email address, no IP address, and no logs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pocket contacts the Trial server only while it has no License key: when it starts, then
      about once an hour and when your Mac wakes until one attempt gets through, and right
      after you remove an Activation.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Trial server runs on Cloudflare. Cloudflare processes your IP address to deliver the
      request and keeps its own aggregate metrics.
    </p>

    <h2 id="lemon-squeezy">Lemon Squeezy: purchase and License</h2>
    <p>
      Lemon Squeezy is our payment provider and merchant of record.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Purchase.</strong> The Buy button opens Lemon Squeezy's checkout page in your
        browser. Lemon Squeezy collects your email address, name, payment and billing details
        there, under its own privacy policy. None of that passes through Pocket, and we never
        see your card details. As the seller, we can see your order in our Lemon Squeezy
        account, including your name and email address.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Activation.</strong> When you enter a License key and activate it, Pocket sends
        Lemon Squeezy the License key and your hashed Mac ID, which is used as the name of the
        Activation. This links the hashed Mac ID to your License in Lemon Squeezy's records.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Daily check.</strong> While Pocket is licensed, it sends the License key and the
        Activation ID to Lemon Squeezy to check that the key is still valid. In normal use this
        happens at most once a day.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Removing an Activation.</strong> When an Activation is removed, by you,
        because you replaced the License key with another one, or because the key turned out
        to belong to a different product, Pocket sends the License key and
        the Activation ID to Lemon Squeezy.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Lemon Squeezy's replies include the buyer's name and email address. Pocket does not read
      or store them. It keeps the License key, the Activation ID and the time of the last check
      in the macOS Keychain.
    </p>

    <h2>Connections you set up yourself</h2>

    <h3>Mail</h3>
    <p>
      If you add a mail account, Pocket connects directly from your Mac to your own mail
      provider, over an encrypted (TLS) connection, with the address and password you enter. It
      sends what a mail client needs to: sign-in, reading and searching your mail (including
      the text you search for), marking messages as read, archiving, and the messages you
      write. Your mail does not pass through our servers.
    </p>
    <p>
      Remote content in emails, such as images and tracking pixels, is blocked by default. If
      you press "Show images" on a message, Pocket loads them from the servers named in that
      email. Those servers then see your IP address and can tell that you opened the message.
      The choice applies to that message only.
    </p>

    <h3 id="assistant">Assistant</h3>
    <p>
      The Assistant is optional. It works only after you connect your own AI provider: your own
      API key for a service such as OpenAI, Anthropic, OpenRouter or Perplexity, a model
      running on your own Mac, or your own Claude or ChatGPT subscription through their
      command-line tool.
    </p>
    <p>When you send the Assistant a message, your provider receives:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>what you typed or dictated, and the recent conversation;</li>
      <li>
        the information needed to answer, when your question calls for it: calendar event
        titles and times, the sender, subject and time of recent or matching emails, note
        titles, playbook names and steps, the current track, and timer state.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Pocket does not add email bodies, note bodies or clipboard contents. Nothing is sent
      until you send a message. All of it goes to your provider under your own account and is
      covered by that provider's privacy policy. We receive none of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      With a Claude or ChatGPT subscription, Pocket runs the provider's command-line tool on
      your Mac. What that tool itself sends is governed by the tool and its provider.
    </p>

    <h2>Other companies Pocket contacts</h2>
    <dl class="parties">
      <dt>Gravatar</dt>
      <dd>
        To show sender pictures in Mail, Pocket asks Gravatar for a picture using an MD5 hash of
        the email address of each person in your message lists and of the sender of a message
        you open. This happens automatically and cannot be turned off at the moment. Each
        address is looked up once and the result is saved on your Mac. An MD5 hash is not strong
        protection: someone who already knows or guesses an address can match it to its hash.
      </dd>

      <dt>Spotify</dt>
      <dd>
        When Spotify is playing and macOS has not already supplied the album cover, Pocket
        downloads the cover from Spotify's image servers. The request contains no personal
        data; the server sees your IP address and which cover was fetched.
      </dd>

      <dt>Apple</dt>
      <dd>
        Dictation uses Apple's speech recognition. Where your language has an on-device model,
        the audio stays on your Mac. Where it does not, Apple's speech recognition sends the
        audio to Apple's servers. Audio is captured only while you are dictating.
      </dd>

      <dt>Servers of remote email images</dt>
      <dd>Only after you press "Show images" on a message, as described under Mail.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h2>What every connection reveals</h2>
    <p>
      Any server Pocket connects to sees your IP address. Requests to the Trial server, Lemon
      Squeezy, Gravatar, Spotify's image servers and an AI provider you connect with an API key
      also carry standard technical headers: the app name and build number, the versions of the
      macOS networking software and system kernel, and your system languages. If Gravatar, Spotify's image
      servers or your AI provider set a cookie, macOS keeps it and sends it back to that same
      service.
    </p>
    <p>
      Links you click in Pocket, such as meeting links, links in emails or the Buy button, open
      in your browser or another app. A playbook you run can open the links and run the
      Shortcuts you put in it; what those do is up to them.
    </p>

    <h2>What Pocket does not contain</h2>
    <p>
      Pocket contains no analytics, no advertising, no crash reporting and no third-party
      software development kits. Crash reports go only to Apple, and only if you have chosen to
      share them with Apple in System Settings. Pocket writes diagnostic entries to your Mac's
      system log; nothing sends them anywhere.
    </p>

    <h2>What Pocket stores on your Mac</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        In the macOS Keychain: your mail password, your AI API key, and your License key with
        its Activation ID.
      </li>
      <li>
        In its cache folder: a list of mail headers (no message bodies) and the sender pictures
        from Gravatar.
      </li>
      <li>Your settings.</li>
      <li>Your notes, as files in the folder you chose.</li>
    </ul>

    {/* Statements about the site itself. Re-check the build output (external
        URLs, scripts, fonts, storage) before changing this section. */}
    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      This website is a set of static pages served by Cloudflare. Cloudflare processes your IP
      address to deliver them. The site has no analytics, sets no cookies, and loads no
      scripts or fonts from other companies.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Download button fetches the Pocket installer from Cloudflare R2, Cloudflare's file
      storage. The Buy button opens Lemon Squeezy's checkout page; what Lemon Squeezy collects
      there is described under
      <a href="#lemon-squeezy">Lemon Squeezy: purchase and License</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How long data is kept</h2>
    <p>
      The Trial entry on our server (the hashed Mac ID and the Trial start date) has no
      automatic expiry. It is kept until we delete it. You can ask us to delete it at any time
      by writing to <a href="mailto:dk2la@icloud.com">dk2la@icloud.com</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Order and License records are kept by Lemon Squeezy under its own privacy policy.
      Everything listed under "What Pocket stores on your Mac" stays there until you delete it.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      If you are in the European Union, the GDPR gives you the right to ask for a copy of the
      data we hold about you and to have it corrected or deleted. Write to
      <a href="mailto:dk2la@icloud.com">dk2la@icloud.com</a>. You can also lodge a complaint
      with your data protection authority. In France, this is the
      <a href="https://www.cnil.fr">CNIL</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If Pocket starts to handle data differently, we will update this page. The date at the
      top shows the latest version.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p><a href="mailto:dk2la@icloud.com">dk2la@icloud.com</a></p>

    <p>See also the <a href="/terms">Terms</a> and the <a href="/refund">Refund Policy</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Third parties as a definition list: reads the same at any width. */
  .parties {
    margin: 0 0 var(--s-3);
  }

  .parties dd {
    margin: 0 0 var(--s-3);
    padding-left: var(--s-3);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
  }
</style>
